Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:02.869 align:start position:0%
 
hi there  steve kaufman  today i'm going to talk about drinking  sake  and language learning  i posted on my blog the linguist on  language  and i talked about how uh in japan very  often when you go to uh  a drinking establishment like an izakaya  and they pour you sake they'll often put  the little glass  the little glass drinking glass inside a  wooden museu which is like a square  cup and when they pour the sake it'll  overflow the little glass and just gets  it collects inside the masu but  basically you're overflowing it and then  you drink from this glass and  you don't really worry about how you end  up collecting all the rest of the sake  because sure enough pretty soon the  waitress or your friend is going to pour  you some more and it'll overflow the  glass and it's a great feeling of  abundance you know we can we got so much  we can waste we're  about you know we're just  overflowing in in the sake and that's  exactly the way i see learning languages  it's a matter of of  letting the words kind of overflow  and and you pick up some and others kind  of  flow past you but they don't really  because they're still there somewhere  you pour again and few more stick and so  forth  and where you you come into contact with  these words of course is in your in your  listening and reading so  you know i posted on this and i had a  number of comments from people some  people liked the image  and felt it reflected that the way they  learned  of course you have the other people who  sort of say well you know if that's the  case why bother saving words you know we  say words that link so why bother saving  them if you're not going to remember  them of course you're not going to  remember them you're going to forget  most of them the first time the second  time the third time but you'll hit them  again and again that's kind of the image  and some people they try to study these  lists of words and they think that if  they study them long enough and if they  have them in their  you know anki system and the ones that  they didn't get will come back again  that they're gonna learn them i actually  don't believe that i think some of the  words you're gonna learn right away and  some of them you're just not gonna learn  them you know or for a long long time  and why that is i don't know and so why  keep torturing yourself with words that  are just hard to learn eventually you'll  come across them in enough context that  that they're going to stick  uh  and so i'm always i guess in these  videos contrasting the sort of just let  it come at you you'll figure it out  approach which is mine  within reason and the other you know i  gotta learn this and i'm gonna learn  this rule i'm gonna learn these words  and i'm gonna get my pronunciation right  i'm gonna nail it all down and have a  solid foundation on which to build the  anti-moon people have this thing you  know pronounce it right from the  beginning or you'll be reinforcing your  mistakes  you know get it right right away and i  just i don't understand that no other  people you gotta be very careful in this  whole language learning thing that you  know i learn certain ways other people  are other ways i can't say that their  way is wrong  but it's not  what works for me  and and i think people have to accept  that if i say it works for me that it  does work for me  and so you'll get people like i again i  had  done  a video in youtube in japanese  and someone said to me how do you deal  with the participles in japanese  and i said i haven't a clue what a  participle is in japanese and i'm sure  most japanese people don't know what a  participle is i manage fine in japanese  so this person comes and came back and  said well if you don't learn the grammar  obviously you can't possibly speak  correctly or something  and someone actually came back and said  well if you don't study the participles  then you must be illiterate in japanese  like why this intolerance  no actually i'm not illiterate in  japanese i read newspapers i read novels  i i write in japanese using uh  you know word processor mind you  and i don't know what a participle is  and it really doesn't matter  it again staying with japanese you know  there are these uh  little expressions in japanese like  um  so  can you teach that no these are things  you just have to kind of get used to  so many things in the language are there  to be discovered and if you discover  them and they eventually stick and  become habits then that's fine  i was up in peace river alberta  and there's a lady there who runs a  chinese restaurant peace river a  thousand people up in northern alberta  and the peace river country  her family has been there for  i don't know 10 15 years the only  chinese family in the community  and she has her little cantonese accent  but  her expressions her use of the word and  she arrived there from china 10 or 14  years ago she's probably late 30s early  40s  and she talks to people on the phone who  are phoning to make arrangements for  whatever they're going to do and her  three kids are off at school and they  come  come in and then she has some teenagers  in the town working and i watch her  communicate she didn't learn that in a  book  she didn't learn about participles but  she's very natural very natural in her  use  of the language and if someone has  suggested her you know get it a hundred  percent right at the beginning if you  don't pronounce it correctly yell for  you forever whatever i mean she was just  given up and gone home so  uh all this to say  once again i mean i'm a bit of a broken  record but people come at me and say  well you must be illiterate  no  there is an approach to language  learning which says just let it all flow  over you read stuff you like  uh  you know there's another one i was  cleaning up my office today  and my swedish you know essentially i'd  forgotten it  although i was born there then i went  there for summer  but i couldn't really use it for  business and i can remember when i  started doing business in sweden i  bought all kinds of books about the wood  business and about forestry and about  the environment and i and so and i was  cleaning up my office and i came across  all these old books that i had bought in  swedish and i remembered of course i  read all these books because i was  interested so reading is is big but so  is listening and just let the words wash  over you and you'll be surprised at how  much you can learn that way without  having to worry about nailing them down  making sure you learned all the words on  your list  making sure you got it right worrying  about how it's pronounced worrying about  what a participle is  and all these funny terms that they come  up with to explain  grammar for japanese and chinese so just  a brief little ramble there and boy you  know that really makes me thirsty i  would really it would be you know  great now to sit down for uh  some sashimi  or you know uni sushi  and have some sake poured in a cup too  until it overflows  but unfortunately i won't have that to  see me thank you for listening bye for  now

